    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Fubini's Theorem and Product Measures [S+N]\n\nFor real Lebesgue integrals over rectangles, exchanging the order of integration is something we *learned* to do in Stage 2 without much justification. The justification is **Fubini's theorem** — and it's much subtler than the analogous Riemann result.\n\n**Tonelli's theorem.** For $f \\ge 0$ measurable on $X \\times Y$ with $\\sigma$-finite measures $\\mu, \\nu$, the iterated integrals are equal:\n\n$$\\int_{X \\times Y} f\\,d(\\mu \\otimes \\nu) = \\int_X \\left(\\int_Y f(x, y)\\,d\\nu(y)\\right) d\\mu(x) = \\int_Y \\left(\\int_X f(x, y)\\,d\\mu(x)\\right) d\\nu(y).$$\n\n**Fubini's theorem.** For real (or complex) measurable $f$ that's integrable on $X \\times Y$ (i.e., $\\int |f|\\,d(\\mu \\otimes \\nu) < \\infty$), the same equality holds.\n\nThe crucial subtlety: **Fubini requires absolute integrability**, not just existence of the iterated integrals. Without it, the two iterated integrals can disagree even when both exist.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Tonelli's theorem in action [S+N]\n\nTake $f(x, y) = e^{-(x^2 + y^2)}$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ — non-negative, so Tonelli applies.\n\n$$\\int\\int e^{-x^2 - y^2}\\,dA = \\left(\\int_{\\mathbb{R}} e^{-x^2}\\,dx\\right)\\left(\\int_{\\mathbb{R}} e^{-y^2}\\,dy\\right) = \\pi.$$\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "I_iterated : integrate(integrate(exp(-x^2 - y^2), x, -inf, inf), y, -inf, inf)$\nprint(\"∫∫ e^{-x²-y²} dA via iteration =\", I_iterated)$\nprint(\"(Tonelli gives π — same as polar Stage 2/05)\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "∫∫ e^{-x²-y²} dA via iteration = %pi\n(Tonelli gives π — same as polar Stage 2/05)"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Fubini fails when $|f|$ isn't integrable\n\nClassic counter-example: $f(x, y) = \\dfrac{x^2 - y^2}{(x^2 + y^2)^2}$ on $[0, 1]^2$.\n\nThe iterated integrals exist *and* differ:\n\n$$\\int_0^1\\!\\int_0^1 f\\,dy\\,dx = \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\quad \\int_0^1\\!\\int_0^1 f\\,dx\\,dy = -\\frac{\\pi}{4}.$$\n\nThe reason: $\\int |f|\\,d(\\mu \\otimes \\nu) = \\infty$ — $f$ has a non-integrable singularity at the origin. Fubini's hypothesis fails, so its conclusion fails.\n\nMaxima can compute both ways:\n",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "f_counter : (x^2 - y^2) / (x^2 + y^2)^2$\ninner_y : integrate(f_counter, y, 0, 1)$\nprint(\"Inner y-integral =\")$\ninner_y;\nouter_x : integrate(inner_y, x, 0, 1)$\nprint(\"∫₀¹ ∫₀¹ f dy dx =\")$\nouter_x;\nprint()$\ninner_x : integrate(f_counter, x, 0, 1)$\nprint(\"Inner x-integral =\")$\ninner_x;\nouter_y : integrate(inner_x, y, 0, 1)$\nprint(\"∫₀¹ ∫₀¹ f dx dy =\")$\nouter_y;",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Inner y-integral =\n1/(x^2+1)\n∫₀¹ ∫₀¹ f dy dx =\n%pi/4\nInner x-integral =\n-(1/(y^2+1))\n∫₀¹ ∫₀¹ f dx dy ="
          ]
        },
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "-\\left({{\\pi}\\over{4}}\\right)"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 6,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "$\\pi/4$ and $-\\pi/4$ — different. The pathology comes from the non-integrable singularity at $(0, 0)$: $\\int |f|\\,d(\\mu\\otimes\\nu)$ diverges, so Fubini doesn't apply.\n\n## Practical recipe\n\nGiven $\\int\\int f\\,dA$:\n\n1. **First check** $\\int |f|\\,dA < \\infty$ (often by Tonelli on $|f|$ — non-negative, so Tonelli always applies).\n2. If yes, **Fubini** says iterated integrals exist, are equal, and equal the double integral.\n3. If no, the iterated integrals may not exist or may disagree — handle case-by-case.\n\nFor everyday integrals of nice continuous functions on bounded regions, this is automatic. For improper integrals or distribution-like behaviour, check absolute integrability first.\n\n## Product measures abstractly\n\nThe $\\sigma$-algebra on $X \\times Y$ is $\\mathcal{F} \\otimes \\mathcal{G}$ — the smallest σ-algebra containing all \"rectangles\" $A \\times B$ with $A \\in \\mathcal{F}$, $B \\in \\mathcal{G}$. The product measure $\\mu \\otimes \\nu$ satisfies $(\\mu \\otimes \\nu)(A \\times B) = \\mu(A)\\,\\nu(B)$ on rectangles, extended via Carathéodory to all measurable sets.\n\n**Important nuance**: the product Lebesgue σ-algebra on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is *strictly contained* in the Lebesgue σ-algebra on $\\mathbb{R}^2$. They agree on Borel sets but differ on null subsets. For most practical purposes the difference doesn't matter.\n\n## Probability flavour: independence\n\nIf $X, Y$ are independent random variables on $(X \\times Y, \\mathcal{F} \\otimes \\mathcal{G}, \\mu \\otimes \\nu)$ with marginal distributions $\\mu_X, \\mu_Y$, then for any (measurable) functions $f, g$,\n\n$$E[f(X) g(Y)] = E[f(X)]\\,E[g(Y)] = \\int f\\,d\\mu_X\\,\\cdot\\int g\\,d\\mu_Y.$$\n\nThis is Tonelli applied to the joint distribution being a product measure. Independence ⇔ joint = product of marginals. Probability theory is just measure theory in dressy clothes.\n\n## What we got from Stage 4\n\nSix notebooks worth of Lebesgue theory:\n\n- **σ-algebras and measures** (notebook 1) — the language.\n- **Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb{R}$** (notebook 2) — the canonical example, with Cantor and Vitali pathologies.\n- **The Lebesgue integral** (notebook 3) — partition the range, not the domain.\n- **Convergence theorems** (notebook 4) — MCT, Fatou, DCT — the workhorses.\n- **$L^p$ spaces** (notebook 5) — complete normed function spaces with Hölder, Minkowski.\n- **Fubini and product measures** (this notebook) — the right rules for iterated integration.\n\nWe're now equipped to do **functional analysis** properly — the abstract study of $L^p$ and similar function spaces. Stage 5 is next.\n",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}